%I A400296 #11 Nov 18 2026 17:34:50
%S A400296 2,5,17,41,127,197,281,379,499,857,953,1151,1259,1583,2099,2399,2417,
%T A400296 2579,2909,3803,3821,4217,4651,5107,5813,6079,6599,7699,8273,8893,
%U A400296 9521,11597,12713,13099,15527,16823,21407,22037,22039,24133,25237,28087,28099,28697,31729,32353,33623,34913
%N A400296 Primes that are the sum of more consecutive primes than any smaller primes.
%H A400296 M. F. Hasler, <a href="/A400296/b400296.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2500</a>
%F A400296 a(n) = min { p = A070281(k); k > A400297(a(n-1)), p > 0 } for n > 1.
%e A400296 a(1) = 2 is the smallest prime, and it is sum of one prime, itself.
%e A400296 a(2) = 5 = 2 + 3 is (obviously the smallest) sum of two consecutive primes.
%e A400296 a(3) = 17 = 2 + 3 + 5 + 7 is the sum of 4 consecutive primes, and no smaller prime is the sum of (at least) three consecutive primes.
%e A400296 a(4) = 41 = 2 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 11 + 13 is the sum of 6 consecutive primes, and no smaller prime is the sum of (at least) five consecutive primes.
%e A400296 a(5) = 127 = 3 + 5 + 7 + 11 + 13 + 17 + 19 + 23 + 29 is the sum of 9 primes, and no smaller prime is the sum of seven or eight primes. (A070281(7) = 197 is larger and A070281(8) = 0 meaning that no 8 consecutive primes sum to a prime.)
%o A400296 (PARI) A400296=List(); my(A=A70281, N=#A-!A[#A], m, k); while(k<N, listput(A400296, m=vecmin([p|p<-A[k++..N], p])); k=vecmax([k|k<-[k..N], A[k]==m])) \\ assuming A70281 is a precomputed initial segment of that sequence
%Y A400296 Increasing subsequence of A070281 (least prime sum of n consecutive primes).
%K A400296 nonn,new
%O A400296 1,1
%A A400296 _M. F. Hasler_, Nov 12 2026
